Subject: How to deal with "Jacobian of the Transform"
Message-ID: <uuqa6LRed5I.xsp9Qmy-tjIJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/uuqa6LRed5I/m/xsp9Qmy-tjIJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Using https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/blob/master/src/models/bugs_examples/vol1/mice/mice.stan the Weibull Mice example, I have a question about when it is necessary to deal with adjustments to the "Jacobian of the Transform".

Imagine that in an application of the Mice model to human survival data, we want to allow the "r" parameters  to vary by cluster (There might be higher infant mortality in some clusters for example), and we want to partially pool information across clusters. "r" is constrained to be positive.

One way to do this, would be to write:

parameters{
...
real R[Z];
real muR;
real<lower=0> sdR;
...}

transformed parameters{
...
real r[Z];
...
for(z in 1:Z)
r[z]<-exp(R[z])
... }

model{
...
R~normal(muR,sdR);

Blah[n] ~ weibull(r[Cluster[n]], exp(-beta / r[Cluster[n]]));
...
}

In this example, should there be an adjustment to the Jacobian? If so what would it be?

If the model was instead specified as:

parameters{
...
real r[Z];
real mur;
real<lower=0> sdr;
...}

model{
...
r~lognormal(mur,sdr);

Blah[n] ~ weibull(r[Cluster[n]], exp(-beta / r[Cluster[n]]));
...
}

Would there be a need to adjust the Jacobian? If so what would it look like?

