Andrew --- this one's up your alley, given that it
seems to involve bounding variance estimates away from
zero.

More below.

On 11/20/13, 9:56 PM, Devin Leopold wrote:
> Sorry to revive this thread I started about a month ago, but I think I might have tracked down the problem with this
> model and should be able to resolve it with just a little help...

Please don't hesitate to write into the list.

> As a recap, I have a simple linear model with a variance structure where I estimate a unique variance for different
> groups (plant species) in the data. As Bob suggested in his original reply, the problem could be that the scale
> parameters for a couple of species (sigma, in the model above) get very small. If I remove the offending species the
> model behaves. I am hoping there is a way to reparameterize the model to deal with this. Is there some other way to
> specify a unique variance for each species that can hand the fact that some get very small? Any help would be greatly
> appreciated.

The simplest thing to do would be to provide a higher
lower bound in:

 >        real<lower = 0, upper = 100> sigma[15]; // unique variance for each species 1-15

This implicitly supposes a Uniform(0,100) prior on sigma,
and that doesn't sound reasonable given the data.

Ideally, you'd be able to fit a hierarchical model.
would look like something like this:

    sigma ~ lognormal(mu_sigma,sigma_sigma);

and then you need priors on parameters mu_sigma and sigma_sigma.
But I have no idea if lognormal() is a good distribution.  You
could just use a regular normal, too, given that the sigma are
already constrained to [0,100].  Or an inverse gamma distribution.
Hopefully, Andrew will weigh in with some more reasonable advice.

- Bob

>
> On Thursday, October 10, 2013 11:57:41 AM UTC-10, Devin Leopold wrote:
>
>     I have been having some trouble implementing a variance structure for a fairly simple linear model of some
>     vegetation cover data using rstan. It is possible I am getting something very basic wrong here and I would
>     appreciate any advise or guidance.
>
>     I have two response variables I would like to model separately (y1 and y2 in the attached data), they are different
>     ways of calculating relative dominance of species in plots. I have drastically simplified the model and data to try
>     and trouble shoot here, but the basic problem remains the same. The data consists of 1573 observations of 15 species
>     in 261 plots, with not all species occurring in all plots. I started by simply modeling the response variable as a
>     linear combination of an intercept, a predictor variable, x, and random effects of species and plot. The problem
>     arises when I attempt to include a variance structure, where the modeled mean response variable has a unique
>     variance for each species (similar to the varIdent parameter in the nlme package in R). Including this variance
>     structure appears to work fine for one of the modeled response variables, but not the other. In the one that does
>     not work I end up with huge Rhat values and low neff (2). When I look at the parameter traces for the model that
>     does not work the chains for most parameters seem to start off mixing, but then drift to separate values and almost
>     completely stop varying during sampling. Interestingly, if I remove the random effect of "plot", but leave in the
>     variance structure (which is based on species) the model seems to behave. I can not seem to figure out why the
>     behavior is so different for the two response variables. The only obvious difference between the two response
>     variables is that the problematic one is much less variable within plots (in some plots all species have the same
>     value, leading to zero variation between species in that plot), but this does not seem to cause a problem when I do
>     not include the variance structure based on species.
>
>     The meat of the stan model looks like this:
>
>     parameters{
>        real alpha;
>        real beta;
>        vector[15] sppran; //random effect of species
>        vector[261] plotran; //random effect of plot
>        real<lower = 0, upper = 100> sigma[15]; // unique variance for each species 1-15
>        real<lower = 0, upper = 100> sispp;
>        real<lower = 0, upper = 100> siplot;
>     }
>
>     transformed parameters{
>        vector[N] mu;
>        vector[N] sigmavector; //create vector of variance values for model block
>        for (i in 1:N){
>          sigmavector[i] <- sigma[genus[i]];
>          mu[i] <- alpha + beta*x[i] + plotran[plot[i]] + genran[genus[i]];
>        }
>     }
>
>     model{
>        alpha ~ normal(0, 1000);
>        beta ~ normal(0, 1000);
>        plotran ~ normal(0, siplot);
>        genran ~ normal(0, sigen);
>        y ~ normal(mu, sigmavector);
>     }
>
>     I have also attached a simple R script and data set that recreates the problem. -Devin
