Ah.  There have been a lot of improvements since 1.3, consider updating!

I'd recommend changing to delta=0.8 (soon to be our new default) and even experiment in increasing that.  Also with a stepsize that small you're probably hitting the max_treedepth threshold, be sure to increase that as necessary.

But really the extremely small stepsize is indicative of a nasty posterior and tweaking your model can help a lot.  More informative priors makes a huge difference, as does non-centered parameterizations (the Matt trick).

On Nov 21, 2013, at 10:48 PM, Devin Leopold <devin....@gmail.com> wrote:

> Micheal - I am still using stan 1.3, so I think the default is delta=0.5, which I did not touch. The eps=1e-4 is the value that stan was getting down to when I let it select the step size (defaults) in the models that failed to achieve good movement/mixing in the chains. I am not exactly sure how to extract the actual acceptance probabilities from the model (I am using rstan). -Devin
>
> On Thursday, November 21, 2013 10:23:28 AM UTC-10, Michael Betancourt wrote:
> > Yeah, the step size is hard to interpret by itself.  What acceptance probability where you getting?
> > It may very well be that you had the kind of hierarchical correlations that required a higher
> > acceptance probability than the default of 0.65 but eps=1e-4 seems extreme.
> >
> > On Nov 21, 2013, at 8:49 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
> >
> > > If the sampler works, that's great.  Any config will give
> > > you proper samples from the posterior.
> > >
> > > You might find it better to let NUTS do the heavy lifting
> > > and just increase the acceptance probability target and
> > > the treedepth if necessary.
> > >
> > > But the bigger question is whether the estimates are
> > > reasonable.  It probably doesn't make sense to have the
> > > sigma for a group be too low.  A hierarchical model will
> > > partially pool the estimates across groups, with how
> > > much pooling determined by how much the groups vary.
> > >
> > > - Bob
> > >
> > > On 11/21/13, 2:32 PM, Devin Leopold wrote:
> > >> Thanks Bob - Through some trial and error I found that if I tweak the sampler parameters I can get the model to converge
> > >> as originally written with good mixing (usually). I was using epsilon=1e-4, equal_step_sizes=T, max_treedepth=16. Not
> > >> surprisingly, this is painfully slow, and some chains occasionally get stuck (though I get high n_eff and Rhat=1 fairly
> > >> quickly if none of the chains get stuck). I am not sure if this brute force approach is acceptable... -Devin
> > >>
> > >> On Thursday, November 21, 2013 9:18:39 AM UTC-10, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> > >>
> > >>    Andrew --- this one's up your alley, given that it
> > >>    seems to involve bounding variance estimates away from
> > >>    zero.
> > >>
> > >>    More below.
> > >>
> > >>    On 11/20/13, 9:56 PM, Devin Leopold wrote:
> > >>     > Sorry to revive this thread I started about a month ago, but I think I might have tracked down the problem with this
> > >>     > model and should be able to resolve it with just a little help...
> > >>
> > >>    Please don't hesitate to write into the list.
> > >>
> > >>     > As a recap, I have a simple linear model with a variance structure where I estimate a unique variance for different
> > >>     > groups (plant species) in the data. As Bob suggested in his original reply, the problem could be that the scale
> > >>     > parameters for a couple of species (sigma, in the model above) get very small. If I remove the offending species the
> > >>     > model behaves. I am hoping there is a way to reparameterize the model to deal with this. Is there some other way to
> > >>     > specify a unique variance for each species that can hand the fact that some get very small? Any help would be
> > >>    greatly
> > >>     > appreciated.
> > >>
> > >>    The simplest thing to do would be to provide a higher
> > >>    lower bound in:
> > >>
> > >>      >        real<lower = 0, upper = 100> sigma[15]; // unique variance for each species 1-15
> > >>
> > >>    This implicitly supposes a Uniform(0,100) prior on sigma,
> > >>    and that doesn't sound reasonable given the data.
> > >>
> > >>    Ideally, you'd be able to fit a hierarchical model.
> > >>    would look like something like this:
> > >>
> > >>         sigma ~ lognormal(mu_sigma,sigma_sigma);
> > >>
> > >>    and then you need priors on parameters mu_sigma and sigma_sigma.
> > >>    But I have no idea if lognormal() is a good distribution.  You
> > >>    could just use a regular normal, too, given that the sigma are
> > >>    already constrained to [0,100].  Or an inverse gamma distribution.
> > >>    Hopefully, Andrew will weigh in with some more reasonable advice.
> > >>
> > >>    - Bob
> > >>
> > >>
