I did try to upgrade to 2.0.1, but discovered a bug with sd() in the transformed parameters block, which I am using in this model, so I had to revert to 1.3 (I reported this to Bob already). You are correct, I did need to increase max_treedepth. I have been using max_treedepth=16, but I am a bit unclear if this is too high (I do not hit it, usually). What is an acceptable amount for the sampler to be hitting the max tree depth? With these changes I have also found the model to be incredibly slow. I tried delta=0.65 max_treedepth=15 before bed last night and still have not finished 4k iterations, now 10hrs later. Is this slow speed expected with settings like this?

I think Andrew was suggesting that I implement the Matt trick on the variance parameter in my model, but I am not completely clear how I would do that. I currently convert my group level variance to a vector that is the length of my data set in the transformed parameters block, so would I simply add a new parameter as a multiplier at this step? I also am not clear what types of priors I would then put on these parameters. Maybe I should also apply the Matt trick to the random effects here? I am really an ecologist, not a statistician, so I apologize if I am slow to understand the details, but I could use some help with the reparameterization. I will repost the simplified version of my model below. Tightening the priors is not a problem, though I tried this alone in the past without noticeable improvement.

parameters{
  real alpha;
  real beta;
  vector[15] sppran; //random effect of species
  vector[261] plotran; //random effect of plot
  real<lower = 0, upper = 100> sigma[15]; // unique variance for each species 1-15
  real<lower = 0, upper = 100> sispp;
  real<lower = 0, upper = 100> siplot;
}

transformed parameters{
  vector[N] mu;
  vector[N] sigmavector; //create vector of variance values for model block

  for (i in 1:N){

    sigmavector[i] <- sigma[genus[i]];  //Should this change to sigmavector[i] <- modifier*sigma[genus[i]]

    mu[i] <- alpha + beta*x[i] + plotran[plot[i]] + genran[genus[i]];
  }
}

model{
  alpha ~ normal(0, 1000);
  beta ~ normal(0, 1000);

  plotran ~ normal(0, siplot);
  genran ~ normal(0, sigen);

  y ~ normal(mu, sigmavector);
}

