From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Sat Nov 23 23:50:46 2013
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Sat, 23 Nov 2013 23:50:46 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Predictions using overdispersed Poisson regression
Message-ID: <SySucsAwvk4.uOWlPZJjj2sJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <SySucsAwvk4.YNK3GD0_cfMJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <SySucsAwvk4.YNK3GD0_cfMJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/SySucsAwvk4/m/uOWlPZJjj2sJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I don't know what the effect is of treating the Poisson's
output as continuous.

You can just code up simple integer predictions like you're
trying to derive directly in the generated quantities block (after the
model) as:

   generated quantities {
     real<lower=0> suic_pred[N_pred];

     for (i in 1:N_pred)

       suic_pred[i] <- poisson_rng(alpha_star

                                   + beta * jahr_centered_pred[i]
                                   + log_bev[i]

                                   + lambda_pred[i]);

   }

You don't need to increment the overall log prob (and in fact can't
from the generated quantities block), because it's not
going to affect the sampling.

It also looks like the variable you called "jahr_centered" is not
centering by subtracting the mean but is rather subtracting
the first element of jahr.

- Bob

