> sigma1[2,4] <- 1; sigma1[4,2] <- sigma1[2,4];
> sigma1[2,5] <- 0; sigma1[5,2] <- sigma1[2,5];
> sigma1[2,6] <- -1; sigma1[6,2] <- sigma1[2,6];
> sigma1[3,4] <- 0; sigma1[4,3] <- sigma1[3,4];
> sigma1[3,5] <- 1; sigma1[5,3] <- sigma1[3,5];
> sigma1[3,6] <- 1; sigma1[6,3] <- sigma1[3,6];
> sigma1[4,5] <- 1; sigma1[5,4] <- sigma1[4,5];
> sigma1[4,6] <- -1; sigma1[6,4] <- sigma1[4,6];
> sigma1[5,6] <- 1; sigma1[6,5] <- sigma1[5,6];

This kind of code is very error prone.  I'd try
to keep the LHS ordered so that for sigma1[i,j]
definitions in the first column you have
i > j.  Then you can make it symmetric with:

  # 1 <= i < j <= 6
  for (i in 1:5)
    for (j in (i+1):6)
       sigma1[i,j] <- sigma1[j,i];

Now that I look at it, this kind of code is very
error prone, too!

> data <- list(sigma1=sigma1)
> fit1 <- stan(model_code=model,data=data,
>               iter=100000,chains=1,thin=1)
>
> # extract samples
> fit_ss <- extract(fit1, permuted = TRUE)
> yy <- fit_ss$y
>

What's the effective sample size and R-hat values?
I'd suggest running way fewer iterations and using
more chains.

